from pathlib import Path
from html import escape
import zipfile

root = Path(__file__).parent
out = root / 'canva-build'
out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
slides = []
def add(title, body, notes, seconds=None, chart=None, caption=None):
    slides.append(dict(title=title, body=body, notes=notes, seconds=seconds, chart=chart, caption=caption))

add('A Hybrid TCP-UDP Transport Protocol for Fragmentation-Free Post-Quantum Voice Chat',
'''<p class="lead">Reliable connection setup. Small encrypted voice packets.</p><p class="subtitle">Review III · Implementation and interim results</p><p class="team">Cain Manoj · 23BCE1663<br>Sreenandu G · 23BCE1120<br>Ashwanth Kumaravel · 23BCE1244</p><p class="guide">Guide: Dr. Renuka Devi S.</p>''',
'''CUE: SETUP + VOICE. Our project is a voice-chat prototype that uses different delivery methods for connection setup and conversation. Post-quantum means the cryptographic algorithms are designed to resist attacks from future quantum computers. We use existing cryptographic libraries. Our contribution is connecting them with the voice transport and evaluating the result. First, let us explain the delivery problem.''',30)
add('Why large messages cause trouble',
'''<p class="lead">A large message may need to travel in several pieces.</p><div class="steps"><p><b>1</b> Connection setup needs large security messages.</p><p><b>2</b> An oversized UDP message can split into IP fragments.</p><p><b>3</b> One missing fragment prevents rebuilding that message.</p></div><p class="takeaway">Think of a puzzle that needs every piece.</p><p class="foot">Our test network allowed 1,500-byte IP packets. Other networks can differ.</p>''',
'''CUE: BIG MESSAGE → PIECES → MISSING PIECE. Before a secure call begins, the devices exchange security information. In our system this information is much larger than a normal network packet. MTU means the largest IP packet a link can carry. Our test uses 1,500 bytes. If we put too much data into one UDP message, it may be split into fragments. The receiver needs all those fragments to rebuild the original message. A missing piece is therefore enough to lose the whole message. This motivates using a different delivery method for setup.''',55)
add('Reliable setup, small voice packets',
'''<div class="transport"><h2>TCP for setup and control</h2><p>Resends missing data so large setup messages can arrive.</p></div><div class="transport"><h2>UDP for the conversation</h2><p>Sends small encrypted voice packets without TCP's delivery waits.</p></div><p class="takeaway">Use each transport for the job it suits.</p><p class="foot">“Hybrid” means TCP + UDP. Voice application datagrams are limited to 1,200 bytes.</p>''',
'''CUE: TCP SETS UP; UDP CARRIES VOICE. TCP provides reliable delivery and resends missing data. That is useful for setup information that must arrive before the call can work. UDP does not provide that same delivery guarantee. It lets us send small voice packets without waiting for TCP to recover earlier missing data. The tradeoff is that some voice packets may be lost. We cap each voice application datagram at 1,200 bytes. This helps avoid oversized voice packets under the tested conditions. Hybrid here means combining TCP and UDP, not combining classical and post-quantum key exchange.''',60)
add('How a call works',
'''<div class="steps"><p><b>1</b> Connect and check the expected peer identity.</p><p><b>2</b> Establish a shared secret and derive the voice key.</p><p><b>3</b> Compress and encrypt the microphone audio.</p><p><b>4</b> Send, decrypt, reorder and play the voice packets.</p></div><p class="takeaway">Connect → establish key → protect voice → play</p><p class="foot">Setup: ML-KEM-768, ML-DSA-65 and HKDF-SHA256. Voice: Opus and ChaCha20-Poly1305.</p>''',
'''CUE: CONNECT → KEY → VOICE → PLAY. First, each device checks signatures against the peer identity key it already trusts. ML-DSA provides those signatures. ML-KEM establishes a shared secret, and HKDF turns that secret into a voice-encryption key. Next, Opus compresses microphone audio, and ChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypts it and checks for tampering. Small voice packets travel over UDP. The receiving device decrypts them, uses a reordering buffer, decodes the audio and plays it. Voice travels both ways. You do not need to explain the mathematics during the main talk. This is a custom academic protocol, not a production-audited calling system.''',60)
add('How we tested the prototype',
'''<p class="lead">We deliberately made the test network less reliable.</p><div class="steps"><p><b>Loss</b> Drop 0% to 30% of packets.</p><p><b>Delay</b> Make packets arrive later and less regularly.</p><p><b>Measure</b> Count fragments, message deliveries and received voice frames.</p></div><p class="takeaway">Compare our hybrid design with all-UDP and TCP baselines.</p><p class="foot">Historical controlled Linux tests. The voice benchmark starts after authentication; audio was not sent at real-time pace.</p>''',
'''CUE: DROP → DELAY → MEASURE. Packet loss means some packets do not arrive. Delay means they arrive later; jitter means that delay varies. We used controlled Linux network tests with real sockets, encryption and Opus encoding. The transport sweep covers zero to thirty percent configured loss, with fifty milliseconds of configured delay and ten milliseconds of jitter. It reports medians from five runs. A separate experiment sends a large synthetic message one hundred times per condition. These are saved historical results. The main voice benchmark authenticates before measurement and does not send audio at real-time pace, so it is not a complete measurement of a live call.''',50)
add('Small packets avoided captured fragments',
'''<p class="stat">0</p><p class="statlabel">fragments observed for hybrid traffic in this capture</p><p class="explain">The oversized UDP baseline produced 4–5 captured fragments.</p><p class="explain">Fewer captured fragments under loss does not mean better delivery.</p>''',
'''CUE: READ AXES → ZERO → LIMIT. Along the bottom is the packet-loss setting. Up the side is the number of captured IP fragments. Our hybrid line stays at zero. The naive, or oversized UDP, baseline shows five fragments at the lower loss settings and four at higher settings. The lower count does not mean the message was delivered better; fragments may be missing from the capture. The important result is that the measured hybrid traffic did not produce captured fragments. The real cryptographic handshake happened before capture, so this graph alone does not prove that the entire call lifecycle is fragmentation-free.''',55,'loss_vs_fragments.png',
'''Captured IP fragments under configured packet loss. The hybrid benchmark capture excludes the actual cryptographic handshake.''')
add('TCP delivered the large test message more often',
'''<p class="eyebrow">AT 20% CONFIGURED PACKET LOSS</p><p class="comparison"><strong>99 / 100</strong><span>TCP deliveries</span></p><p class="comparison"><strong>19 / 100</strong><span>oversized UDP deliveries</span></p><p class="explain">Reliable delivery helps, but resending data takes time.</p>''',
'''CUE: 20% LOSS → 99 VERSUS 19 → TEST MESSAGE. The horizontal axis shows configured packet loss. The vertical axis shows the percentage of large test messages delivered. At twenty percent loss, TCP delivered ninety-nine out of one hundred, while naive UDP delivered nineteen. TCP can resend missing data. UDP sent its large message once. This is not a count of successfully authenticated calls. The original chart uses handshake wording, but the experiment only sends a synthetic message containing the same number of bytes as the cryptographic material. Also, the waiting policies differ: UDP has a one-second timeout, while TCP uses twenty-second wait settings. The result shows delivery under these policies, not an equal-time comparison. TCP gains reliability at the cost of possible waiting.''',70,'handshake_survival.png',
'''Handshake-sized payload delivery, not authenticated call success. Synthetic 10,842-byte payload; 100 attempts per condition. UDP: 1-second timeout. TCP: 20-second wait settings.''')
add('Voice estimates fall as packets go missing',
'''<p class="lead">Higher MOS means a higher estimated quality score.</p><p class="explain">Hybrid and ordinary UDP give similar estimates.</p><p class="explain">TCP's flat line does not prove better live conversation.</p><p class="takeaway">This is a model estimate, not a listening test.</p>''',
'''CUE: HIGHER SCORE → SIMILAR UDP LINES → MODEL LIMIT. MOS stands for Mean Opinion Score. Here it is calculated from a simplified model; people did not listen and rate the calls. As loss increases, the hybrid and naive UDP estimates both fall and remain close to each other. Small voice packets do not have the same oversized-message problem as setup traffic. The TCP line stays high because it delivers the frames, but the model does not fully capture the cost of waiting for late voice data. We therefore cannot claim that TCP gives a better live conversation, or that our hybrid system improves perceived voice quality. Our strongest current results concern packet sizing and delivery of large messages.''',65,'loss_vs_mos.png',
'''Model-estimated MOS using simplified assumptions. Not a listening study or a complete playback-latency measurement.''')
add('What we have shown, and what comes next',
'''<h2>Implemented</h2><p class="summary">Post-quantum setup over TCP and encrypted voice over UDP.</p><h2>Observed</h2><p class="summary">Zero captured hybrid fragments and stronger large-message delivery over TCP.</p><h2>Next</h2><p class="summary">Measure full authenticated setup and real-time audio under controlled loss.</p><p class="takeaway">Next: our separate live demonstration</p>''',
'''CUE: BUILT → MEASURED → NEXT. We built the transport integration and encrypted voice prototype. Our controlled results show zero captured fragments in hybrid benchmark traffic and stronger delivery of a large test message using TCP. Next, we need complete authenticated-setup measurements with a common deadline, realistically paced audio tests and integration of the separate recovery method into the live receiver. We will now show our two-minute live demonstration. STOP THE MAIN DECK HERE. Slides 10 to 13 are for questions only.''',35)
add('Backup · Recovering missing audio',
'''<p class="lead">Extra recovery information can help reconstruct missing audio.</p><p class="explain">The recovery experiment performed better than filling gaps with silence.</p><p class="takeaway">This recovery experiment is separate from the live receiver.</p>''',
'''CUE: RECOVERY EXPERIMENT, NOT LIVE FEATURE. FEC means forward error correction. The tested decoder attempts to use information in the next packet to recover a missing frame. The graph shows signal-to-noise ratio, or SNR: higher means closer to the undamaged decoded reference. This used a three-second generated signal, not recorded speech. The comparison fills unrecovered gaps with zeros, not an optimized speech-concealment method. The 99 dB value is a numerical cap when no error is measured. The attempt counter does not independently verify that a packet contained redundancy. These findings do not prove perceived speech improvement, and this recovery behavior is not integrated into the current live receive path.''',chart='fec_recovery.png',caption='Separate reconstruction experiment on a generated signal, compared with silence filling. SNR is not a listener rating. Not the live receiver’s recovery behavior.')
add('Backup · Time to the first received voice frame',
'''<p class="lead">More network delay means a longer wait for the first frame.</p><p class="explain">Timing starts when voice sending begins.</p><p class="takeaway">It excludes connection setup and does not measure the time until a person hears audio.</p>''',
'''CUE: FIRST RECEIVED FRAME, NOT FULL CALL DELAY. The horizontal axis is the configured network delay, and the vertical axis is time to the first received voice frame in milliseconds. TTFB is shorthand for time to first byte in the image. For this prototype benchmark, use the precise first-received-frame definition. The cryptographic handshake is completed before this timer begins. Frames are sent without real-time pacing. The figure does not measure microphone-to-speaker delay or complete call setup. Do not compare its numbers directly with the ns-3 timing graph, because the models use different timing definitions.''',chart='latency_vs_ttfb.png',caption='Sending-start to first received voice frame. Excludes the cryptographic handshake; benchmark audio is not paced in real time.')
add('Backup · Simulated voice-quality estimates',
'''<p class="lead">A separate network model also estimates quality under packet loss.</p><p class="explain">The QUIC line represents a simplified proxy.</p><p class="takeaway">Supporting model results, not measurements of a real QUIC calling system.</p>''',
'''CUE: SIMULATION ONLY. ns-3 is a network simulator. This is a separate modeled comparison, not another real-prototype measurement. Its MOS scores are estimated. The QUIC path is simplified and is not a real QUIC implementation. The TCP model does not fully represent playback delays, which limits what we can infer from its high score. Use this graph as supporting evidence only, rather than ranking actual calling applications.''',chart='ns3_loss_vs_mos.png',caption='Secondary ns-3 model: estimated MOS under loss. QUIC is a simplified proxy; TCP playback delay is not fully represented.')
add('Backup · Simulated first-byte timing',
'''<p class="lead">The modeled transports use different setup and delivery behavior.</p><p class="explain">The lower QUIC-proxy line belongs to this simulation.</p><p class="takeaway">It does not establish that real QUIC calls are faster by this amount.</p>''',
'''CUE: DIFFERENT MODEL, DIFFERENT TIMER. This graph reports first-byte timing in the ns-3 model. The simplified QUIC proxy has lower modeled timing here. That is a result of the modeled behavior and assumptions, not a measurement of a deployed QUIC application. TCP latency accounting has limitations. Do not compare the absolute values with the prototype first-frame chart: the two experiments time different portions of their processes. A fair end-to-end comparison remains future work.''',chart='ns3_latency_vs_ttfb.png',caption='Secondary ns-3 modeled first-byte timing. QUIC is a simplified proxy. Do not compare directly with the differently timed real-prototype benchmark.')

css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#e8edf3;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:#172d49}.slide{width:1600px;height:900px;background:white;position:relative;padding:65px 82px;margin:24px auto;overflow:hidden;border-top:12px solid #276fa2}h1{font-size:54px;line-height:1.12;margin:0 0 42px;max-width:1420px;letter-spacing:-1px}h2{font-size:35px;margin:25px 0 14px;color:#276fa2}p{margin:0 0 24px;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35}.lead{font-size:39px;line-height:1.3}.steps p{font-size:34px;margin:30px 0}.steps b{color:#276fa2;display:inline-block;min-width:88px}.takeaway{font-size:32px;color:#276fa2;font-weight:bold;margin-top:30px}.foot{position:absolute;bottom:35px;left:82px;right:105px;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;color:#526072}.number{position:absolute;bottom:22px;right:40px;font-size:20px;color:#68768a}.cover h1{font-size:59px;max-width:1390px;margin-bottom:34px}.subtitle{font-size:26px;color:#526072}.team{font-size:27px;line-height:1.65;margin-top:32px}.guide{font-size:25px}.transport{margin-bottom:55px;max-width:1250px}.transport p{font-size:35px}.chart h1{font-size:49px;margin-bottom:20px}.chartwrap{position:absolute;left:65px;top:157px;width:820px;height:590px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center}.chartwrap img{max-width:100%;max-height:100%;object-fit:contain}.charttext{position:absolute;left:940px;top:175px;width:565px}.charttext p{font-size:30px}.charttext .lead{font-size:34px}.charttext .stat{font-size:115px;line-height:1;color:#276fa2;margin-bottom:10px}.statlabel{font-size:34px!important}.charttext .explain{margin-top:25px}.chart .foot{bottom:45px;font-size:21px;left:82px;right:90px}.eyebrow{font-size:22px!important;color:#526072}.comparison strong{font-size:65px;display:block;color:#276fa2}.comparison span{display:block;font-size:28px}.summary{font-size:32px;max-width:1330px}.charttext .takeaway{font-size:29px}.cover .lead{font-size:35px}'''
pages=[]
for i,s in enumerate(slides,1):
    classes='slide'+(' cover' if i==1 else '')+(' chart' if s['chart'] else '')
    content=s['body']
    if s['chart']:
        content=f'<div class="chartwrap"><img src="charts/{s["chart"]}" alt="{escape(s["caption"],quote=True)}"></div><div class="charttext">{content}</div><p class="foot">{escape(s["caption"])}</p>'
    pages.append(f'<section class="{classes}" data-document-role="page" data-label="{escape(s["title"],quote=True)}" data-speaker-notes="{escape(s["notes"],quote=True)}"><h1>{escape(s["title"])}</h1>{content}<span class="number">{i:02d}</span></section>')
html='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Review III · Hybrid post-quantum voice transport</title><style>'+css+'</style></head><body>'+''.join(pages)+'</body></html>'
(out/'index.html').write_text(html)
notes=['# Review III speaking guide','Main slides: 8 minutes. Separate live demo: 2 minutes. Backup slides are for questions.','Memory route: Problem → Solution → Call → Tests → Fragments → Delivery → Voice → Conclusion.']
for i,s in enumerate(slides,1):
    notes += [f'## {i}. {s["title"]}',f'Target: {s["seconds"]} seconds.' if s['seconds'] else 'Backup only.',s['notes']]
(root/'PRESENTER-NOTES.md').write_text('\n\n'.join(notes)+'\n')
with zipfile.ZipFile(out/'review-iii-canva.zip','w',zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as z:
    z.write(out/'index.html','index.html')
    for s in slides:
        if s['chart']: z.write(root/'charts'/s['chart'],'charts/'+s['chart'])
assert len(slides)==13 and sum(s['seconds'] or 0 for s in slides)==480
assert len({s['chart'] for s in slides if s['chart']})==7
print(out/'review-iii-canva.zip')
print('Verified: 13 slides, 7 original charts, 480 seconds main-slide timing, speaker notes on every slide.')
